#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e891363-fea2-5de4-9bbb-67fddb077200" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/id #uuid "51467bae-f181-53ba-adf2-1218dc16275f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7024" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=0$, $y=0$, and the curve $y=\\sqrt{4 - x^2}$. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2151/Source/Images/14f102586d04e8d0746c319f2ae457a6.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{4-x^2} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(x + y)dA &= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}(x + y)dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853b2770-5057-50aa-9c15-7426cf4cc6a4" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "7f443df5-1c26-5af4-9541-e83522126197" :question/math-academy-id "e-7025" :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region in the first quadrant enclosed by the curves $y=3x$ and $y=x^3$, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left({x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2151/Source/Images/4436a12852e62e082097841fa7fa1561.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region.\n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x$:\n$x^{3}|= 3x; x^{3} - 3x|= 0; x(x^{2} - 3)|= 0$\nSo, $x$-limits are $0 \\le x \\le \\sqrt{3}$. Note that we disregarded the solution $x=-\\sqrt 3$ since $x\\geq 0$ in our region.\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2151/Source/Images/f0558707f61014c153cc8376fd9a9f38.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{3}, \\quad x^3 \\leq y \\leq 3x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}∫_{x^{3}}^{3x}f(x, y)dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}[∫_{x^{3}}^{3x}f(x, y)dy]dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/id #uuid "b151be22-91a1-5303-b86d-4f9ba748f000" :question/math-academy-id "e-7026" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_0^x x^2y \\,\\textrm{d}y \\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}x^{2}ydydx &= ∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}[∫_{0}^{x}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}[\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}x^{2}(\\frac{x^{2}}{2} - 0)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{1}x^{4}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{x^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133574 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/id #uuid "24dd94c8-2821-5614-9ccf-43c3656267f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7027" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region below the $x$-axis enclosed by the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4$." :question/worked-solution "Since\n\n$$\ny \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 4 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y = -\\sqrt{4-x^2}\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2151/Source/Images/3a70282170b649d4c76265cefd90012b.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the circle with the line $y=0$.\nSo, the $x$-limits are\n$-2 \\le x \\le 2$.\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2151/Source/Images/5833d31c6d9bf6891307c40c65594659.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD = \\Big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\quad -\\sqrt{4-x^2} \\leq y \\leq 0 \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-2}^2 \\int_{-\\sqrt{4-x^2}}^{0} xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-2}^{2}∫_{-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}^{0}xydydx &= ∫_{-2}^{2}x[∫_{-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}^{0}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{2}x[\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}^{0}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{-2}^{2}x(0 - (-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}})^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{-2}^{2}x(- (4 - x^{2}))dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{-2}^{2}x(x^{2} - 4)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{-2}^{2}(x^{3} - 4x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}∫_{-2}^{2}(x^{3} - 4x)dx &= \\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{x^{4}}{4} - 2x^{2}]_{-2}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}((4 - 8) - (4 - 8)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ece16374-22bf-5321-ac45-d500b6f255cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131139} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b7f34f-c128-5ad9-80a0-a72dd60d5f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131140} {:answer/id #uuid "53badbc5-d6c3-52a1-987f-5cd8886a822b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131141} {:answer/id #uuid "5976a1fa-56cc-537e-ba7e-1d9bb0f41ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131142} {:answer/id #uuid "69aaf2c2-d699-580e-bebe-32b2a7ceff97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ece16374-22bf-5321-ac45-d500b6f255cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131139} :answer-field/id #uuid "6263d9f8-f4af-5e72-bf91-f5495a3cccbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131138}] :question/id #uuid "1b47314b-c252-595b-b8b8-d7b952718a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-37863" :question/problem "Calculate $∬_(D)\\sin (x + y)dA$, where $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le \\frac{π}{2}, 0 \\le y \\le x}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"}] :question/answer-fie